    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i think i see what you mean about the total, but i'm a bit confused on the logic there. if the total is just a sum of the items, shouldn't we just calculate it on the fly instead of storing it? also, what's the best way to actually structure the new tables once we find these dependencies so we don't end up with even more mess?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're correct that in some cases, calculating totals on the fly can be a better approach than storing them, especially if the total is a simple aggregation of other values.\n\nTo avoid creating more mess, it's essential to design your new tables carefully. \n\nIn the Order_items example, you could introduce a new 3NF table, `Order_itemsDetails`, with columns for `order_id`, `item_id`, `price`, and `quantity`. Another 3NF table, `Order_totals`, would contain a composite key of `order_id` and the `total`. The `Order_totals` table would have a single column for `total`. \n\nHowever, in today's data warehousing and reporting, you may still want to store the order totals. You could then strap a foreign key to `Order_totals` ( perhaps from `Orders`) to link directly the orders' totals back into the higher-level `orders` table.\n\nThis way, you can have a safer 3NF database structure.",
      "source": "target",
      "gen_token_ids": [
        2675,
        2351,
        4495,
